{{Short description|Representation of Arabic in Latin script}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=August 2022}}
[[File:Google Ngrams chart showing the changing Romanization of Arabic vowels.jpg|thumb|upright=2|[[Google Ngrams]] chart showing the changing English romanization of the [[Arabic phonology|Arabic short vowels]] between the 19th and 20th centuries, using the words ''[[Muslim]]'' and ''[[Muhammad]]'' as examples|class=skin-invert-image]]
{{Arabic-script sidebar}}

The '''romanization of Arabic''' is the systematic rendering of [[Modern Standard Arabic|written]] and [[varieties of Arabic|spoken]] [[Arabic language|Arabic]] in the [[Latin script]]. Romanized Arabic is used for various purposes, among them transcription of names and titles, [[cataloging]] Arabic language works, [[language education]] when used instead of or alongside the Arabic script, and representation of the language in scientific publications by [[Linguistics|linguists]]. These formal systems, which often make use of [[diacritics]] and non-standard Latin characters, are used in academic settings for the benefit of non-speakers, contrasting with informal means of written communication used by speakers such as the Latin-based [[Arabic chat alphabet]].

Different systems and strategies have been developed to address the inherent problems of rendering various Arabic varieties in the Latin script. Examples of such problems are the symbols for Arabic [[phoneme]]s that do not exist in [[English language|English]] or other [[Europe]]an languages; the means of representing the [[Arabic definite article]], which is always spelled the same way in written Arabic but has numerous pronunciations in the spoken language depending on context; and the representation of short vowels (usually ''i&nbsp;u'' or ''e&nbsp;o'', accounting for variations such as ''[[Muslim]] and Moslem'' or ''[[Mohammed]], Muhammad and Mohamed'').

==Method==
Romanization is often termed "transliteration", but this is not technically correct (especially for [[Abjad|abjads]]).{{citation needed|date=July 2014}} [[Transliteration]] is the direct representation of foreign ''letters'' using Latin symbols, while most systems for romanizing Arabic are actually [[Phonetic transcription|transcription]] systems, which represent the ''sound'' of the language, since short vowels and geminate consonants, for example, do not usually appear in Arabic writing. As an example, the following rendering "''{{transliteration|ar|ALA|munāẓaratu l-ḥurūfi l-ʿarabiyyah}}''" of {{Langx|ar|مناظرة&nbsp;الحروف&nbsp;العربية}} is a transcription, indicating the pronunciation; an example of transliteration would be ''{{transliteration|ar|mnaẓrḧ&nbsp;alḥrwf&nbsp;alʻrbyḧ}}''.

== Romanization standards and systems ==
Principal standards and systems are:

===Early Romanization===
[[File:1506 Arabic transcription - Arte para ligeramente saber la lengua arauiga.jpg|thumb|200px|[[Pedro de Alcalá|De Alcalá]]'s work has been called the "first Western system of Arabic scientific transcription".<ref>{{cite book|last1=Corriente |first1=Francisco |title=The Andalusian Arabic lexicon according Pedro de Alcalá |date=1988 |location=Madrid}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Soto González |first1=Teresa |title=The language of ordinary people, not the priors of Arabic grammar |journal=Ilu. Journal of Religious Studies |date=2021-05-31 |volume=24 |issue=2019 |pages=125-141 |doi=10.5209/ilur.75206 |url=https://revistas.ucm.es/index.php/ILUR/article/view/75206}}</ref>]]

Early Romanization of the Arabic language was standardized in the various [[List of Arabic dictionaries#Bilingual dictionaries|bilingual Arabic-European dictionaries]] of the 17–19th centuries:
* [[Pedro de Alcalá]], ''Vocabulista'', 1505. A Spanish{{ndash}}Arabic glossary using a systematic transcription.<ref name=Lipiński>[[Edward Lipiński (orientalist)|Edward Lipiński]], 2012, [http://cejsh.icm.edu.pl/cejsh/element/bwmeta1.element.hdl_11089_2963/c/No_2_2012.21-47.pdf Arabic Linguistics: A Historiographic Overview], pages 32–33</ref>
* [[Valentin Schindler]], ''Lexicon Pentaglotton: Hebraicum, Chaldicum, Syriacum, Talmudico-Rabbinicum, et Arabicum'', 1612. Arabic lemmas were printed in Hebrew characters.<ref name=Lipiński/>
* [[Franciscus Raphelengius]], [https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_84yAEE271K8C ''Lexicon Arabicum''], Leiden 1613. The first printed dictionary of the Arabic language in Arabic characters.<ref name=Lipiński/>
* [[Jacobus Golius]], ''Lexicon Arabico-Latinum'', Leiden 1653. The dominant Arabic dictionary in Europe for almost two centuries.<ref name=Lipiński/>
* [[Georg Freytag]], ''Lexicon Arabico-Latinum'', praesertim ex Djeuharii Firuzubadiique et aliorum libris confectum I–IV, Halle 1830–1837<ref name=Lipiński/>
* [[Edward William Lane]], ''[[Arabic–English Lexicon]]'', 8 vols, London-Edinburgh 1863–1893. Highly influential, but incomplete (stops at Kaf)<ref name=Lipiński/>

===Mixed digraphic and diacritical ===
{{further|Digraph (orthography)|Diacritic}}
* '''[[BGN/PCGN romanization|BGN/PCGN]]''' romanization (1956).<ref name=BGN-PCGN>{{Cite web|title=Romanization system for Arabic. BGN/PCGN 1956 System|url=https://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/320079/Arabic_Romanization.pdf}}</ref>
* '''[[UNGEGN]]''' (1972). United Nations Group of Experts on Geographical Names,  or "Variant A of the Amended Beirut System". Adopted from BGN/PCGN.<ref name=UNGEGN>{{Cite web|url=http://www.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_ar.pdf|title=Arabic|publisher=UNGEGN}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|title=Technical reference manual for the standardization of geographical names|year=2007|url=http://unstats.un.org/unsd/geoinfo/UNGEGN/docs/pubs/UNGEGN%20tech%20ref%20manual_m87_combined.pdf |publisher=UNGEGN|page=12 [22]}}</ref>
** '''[[Institut Géographique National|IGN]] System 1973''' or "Variant B of the Amended Beirut System", that conforms to the French orthography and is preferred to the Variant A in French-speaking countries as in Maghreb and Lebanon.<ref name=UNGEGN /><ref>{{Cite web|title=Systèmes français de romanisation|url=http://unstats.un.org/unsd/geoinfo/ungegn/docs/25th-gegn-docs/wp%20papers/crp3.pdf|publisher=UNGEGN|date=2009}}</ref>
** '''{{abbr|ADEGN|Arab delegations to the Eighth UN Conference on the Standardization of Geographical Names in Berlin (2002)}}''' romanization (2007) is different from UNGEGN in two ways: (1) ظ is d͟h instead of z̧; (2) the cedilla is replaced by a sub-macron (_) in all the characters with the cedilla.<ref name=UNGEGN/>
* '''[[ALA-LC]]''' (first published 1991), from the [[American Library Association]] and the [[Library of Congress]].<ref name=ALA-LC>{{Cite web|url=https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/arabic.pdf |title=Arabic romanization table|publisher=The Library of Congress}}</ref> This romanization is close to the romanization of the {{Lang|de|[[Deutsche Morgenländische Gesellschaft]]}} and [[Hans Wehr]], which is used internationally in scientific publications by [[Arabist]]s.
** '''IJMES''', used by ''International Journal of Middle East Studies'', very similar to ALA-LC.<ref>{{Cite web|title=IJMES Translation & Transliteration Guide|url=http://ijmes.chass.ncsu.edu/IJMES_Translation_and_Transliteration_Guide.htm|publisher=International Journal of Middle East Studies|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021063826/http://ijmes.chass.ncsu.edu/IJMES_Translation_and_Transliteration_Guide.htm|archive-date=2014-10-21}}</ref>
** '''EI''', ''[[Encyclopaedia of Islam]]'' (1st ed., 1913–1938; 2nd ed., 1960–2005).<ref name="EI">{{Cite web|title=Encyclopaedia of Islam Romanization vs ALA Romanization for Arabic|website=University of Washington Libraries|url=http://guides.lib.uw.edu/c.php?g=341351&p=2970796}}</ref>

=== Fully diacritical ===
* '''DMG''' (''{{Lang|de|[[Deutsche Morgenländische Gesellschaft]]}}'', 1935), adopted by the International Convention of Orientalist Scholars in Rome.<ref name=DGM1935>{{Cite book |last1=Brockelmann |first1=Carl |last2=Ronkel |first2=Philippus Samuel van |author-link1=Carl Brockelmann |title=Die Transliteration der arabischen Schrift... |date=1935 |location=Leipzig |url=http://www.naher-osten.uni-muenchen.de/studium_lehre/werkzeugkasten/dmgtransliteration.pdf }}</ref>
** '''[[DIN 31635]]''' (1982), developed by the German Institute for Standardization (''{{Lang|de|[[Deutsches Institut für Normung]]}}'').
** '''[[Hans Wehr transliteration]]''' (1961, 1994), a modification to DIN 31635.
** '''EALL''', ''Encyclopedia of Arabic Language and Linguistics'' (edited by Kees Versteegh, Brill, 2006–2009).<ref name="EALL">{{Cite book |title=Encyclopedia of Arabic Language and Linguistics |last=Reichmuth |first=Philipp |publisher=Brill |year=2009 |editor-last=Versteegh |editor-first=Kees |volume=4 |pages=515–20 |chapter=Transcription }}</ref>
** Spanish romanization, identical to DMG/DIN with the exception of three letters: ǧ > ŷ, ḫ > j, ġ > g.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Millar |first1=M. Angélica |last2=Salgado |first2=Rosa |last3=Zedán |first3=Marcela |title=Gramatica de la lengua arabe para hispanohablantes |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=FE8w5YF7650C&pg=PA53 |year=2005 |publisher=Editorial Universitaria |location=Santiago de Chile |isbn=978-956-11-1799-0 |pages=53–54 }}</ref>
* '''[[ISO 233]]''' (1984), letter-to-letter; vowels are transliterated only if they are shown with diacritics, otherwise they are omitted.
** [[:fr:ISO 233-2|ISO 233-2]] (1993), simplified transliteration; vowels are always shown.{{Verify source|date=October 2015}}
* [[BS 4280]] (1968), developed by the [[BSI Group|British Standards Institution]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.bsi-global.com/index.xalter |title=Standards, Training, Testing, Assessment and Certification |publisher=[[BSI Group]] |access-date=18 May 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081007063234/http://www.bsi-global.com/index.xalter |archive-date=7 October 2008}}</ref>

=== ASCII-based ===
* [[ArabTeX]] (since 1992) has been modelled closely after the transliteration standards [[ISO/R 233]] and DIN 31635.<ref>[http://129.69.218.213/arabtex/html/arabdoc.pdf ArabTex User Manual] Section 4.1 : ASCII Transliteration Encoding.</ref>
* [[Buckwalter Transliteration]] (1990s), developed at ALPNET by [[Tim Buckwalter]]; does not require [[diacritic]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.qamus.org/transliteration.htm |title=Buckwalter Arabic Transliteration |publisher=QAMUS LLC}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://open.xerox.com/Services/arabic-morphology/Pages/buckwalter-about |title=Arabic Morphological Analyzer/The Buckwalter Transliteration |publisher=Xerox |access-date=30 April 2017 }}</ref>
* [[Arabic chat alphabet|Arabic '''chat''' alphabet]]:<ref name="EALL" /> an ad hoc solution for conveniently entering Arabic using a Latin keyboard.

